            "text": "An analysis of the execution envelope shows that computing pairwise Euclidean distances directly by broadcasting $(x_i - x_j)^2$ across all $8000 \\times 8000 \\times 1024$ coordinates requires over 130 GB of memory bandwidth and $1.3 \\times 10^{11}$ operations, exceeding the 3-second wall-time limit on 1 CPU core. Furthermore, multi-threaded BLAS libraries defaulting to host core counts suffer massive CPU throttling and context-switching overhead under a 1-core quota.\n\nTo satisfy the 3-second limit and the 1024 MiB memory budget:\n1. Restrict BLAS execution to a single thread via environment variables before importing NumPy.\n2. Formulate the distance calculation via matrix multiplication $G = X X^T$ using highly-optimized single-core BLAS GEMM (operating in CPU cache).\n3. Mean-center the vectors before computing inner products to prevent catastrophic cancellation.\n4. Update the distance matrix in-place to stay well within the memory ceiling.\n\n```python\nimport os\n\nos.environ[\"OMP_NUM_THREADS\"] = \"1\"\nos.environ[\"OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS\"] = \"1\"\nos.environ[\"MKL_NUM_THREADS\"] = \"1\"\nos.environ[\"VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS\"] = \"1\"\nos.environ[\"NUMEXPR_NUM_THREADS\"] = \"1\"\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef main():\n    X = np.load(\"vectors_secondary.npy\")\n\n    # Center vectors to maximize numerical stability of ||a - b||^2 = ||a||^2 + ||b||^2 - 2<a,b>\n    X -= np.mean(X, axis=0, keepdims=True)\n\n    sq_norms = np.sum(X**2, axis=1)\n\n    # Compute pairwise inner products using GEMM\n    G = np.dot(X, X.T)\n\n    # Transform in-place to squared Euclidean distances: ||x_i - x_j||^2 = ||x_i||^2 + ||x_j||^2 - 2(x_i . x_j)\n    G *= -2.0\n    G += sq_norms[:, None]\n    G += sq_norms[None, :]\n\n    # Clamp precision artifacts and ensure exact zero diagonal\n    np.maximum(G, 0.0, out=G)\n    np.fill_diagonal(G, 0.0)\n\n    # Compute Euclidean distance\n    np.sqrt(G, out=G)\n\n    total = float(np.sum(G, dtype=np.float64))\n    